   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 831 of the laws of 1981, amending the labor law\nrelating to fees and expenses in unemployment insurance proceedings, in\nrelation to extending the provisions of such chapter",
    "basePrintNo": "S8653",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8653-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 831 of 1981",
    "lawSection": "Labor",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8653\n\nSPONSOR: MURPHY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 831 of the laws of 1981, amending the labor law\nrelating to fees and expenses in unemployment insurance proceedings, in\nrelation to extending the provisions of such chapter\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would extend subdivisions (3)(a) and (3)(c) of\nLabor Law § 538 until December 31, 2020.\n\nSection two of the bill would make it effective immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLabor Law § 538 (3)(a) requires agents for claimants appearing before\nthe Unemployment Insurance Appeals Board (\"UIAB\") to, among other\nthings, register their contact information with the UIAB and post a $500\nbond for the benefit of claimants charged certain fees by that agent. It\nalso requires agents for employers to present the UIAB with a letter\nproving that they are in fact authorized to represent the employer.\n\nLabor Law Section § 538 (3)(c) requires the UIAB to maintain a list of\nauthorized agents and attorneys available to represent claimants and\nprovide this list to claimants upon request.\n\nThese provisions ensure that claimants are properly represented and not\ncharged an undue fee. It is important to ensure that New Yorkers appear-\ning before the UIAB continue to be represented by qualified agents at\nfair rates.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis provision has been routinely extended since it was first enacted in\n1981 (L. 1981, c. 831; L. 1984, c. 278; L. 1988, c. 278; L.1992, c. 129;\nL.1996, c. 590; L.1998, c. 490; L.2000, c. 339; L.2003, c.  307; L.2004,\nc. 704; L. 2006, c. 332; L.2008, c. 634; L.2010, c. 257; L.2012, c. 331;\nL.2014, c. 115; L.2016, c. 135).\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
